ntake/team-impl-log.md:98:  - artifacts/03-message-projection-tokens/006-verify.json (revise: F-03-001 resolved, F-03-002 lazy-migrate on arbitrary files)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:99:  - artifacts/03-message-projection-tokens/008-verify.json (pass: both resolved)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:149:- Story-lead routed fix work to quick-fix (Opus) despite verifier recommendedFixScope same-session-implementor — worked here, flagged for lspec-core review.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:151:- Post-cycle review queue: consider ESLint upgrade for lint gate; unknown_command CLI-adapter code; stub op-name spelling; hash-manifest immutability mechanism.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:166:- Story 0: scaffold, errors.ts vocabulary (caller_error/state_corruption/system_error; codes path_exists, thread_not_found, invalid_event, empty_batch, empty_stdin, turn_state_corrupt, storage_failure), token-counting util (TOKEN_ESTIMATOR_ID \"js-tiktoken:o200k_base\"), CLI fail-closed stubs, fixture builders, four gates + boundary script, gate self-test FC-0.7. Owns FC-0.1..0.7, no epic ACs.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:168:- Story 2: heaviest, full-staged-risk. Validation precedes idempotency; skips consume no order numbers; key-wins-over-content; dense MAX(event_order). Four arch-risk tests: mid-walk rollback, restart survival, no-lock-on-rejection, system_error rollback parity. Closes TC-1.4. TC-4.4 corruption leg deferred to S4.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:169:- Story 3: projection in same walk iteration/transaction; verbatim content (300KB byte-identical, SDK + spawned CLI); turnId stays null; projection failure rejects batch.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:170:- Story 4: pure transition fn golden per rule table; transition-then-stamp order; corruption check once at state load; gap messages null forever; corrupt fixture = one sanctioned below-SDK write; completes TC-4.4. AC-3.6/work halves deferred to S5 (close paths simply don't call queue; nothing stubbed).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:171:- Story 5: work-queue util domain-blind, no public SDK surface; kind gate prompt->prompt_smoothing, tool_result->tool_result_summary (messages), turn close->turn_derivation (turns); ids w-<sourceId>-<kind>; gated on recorded. Named exit step: WorkItemRecord field-by-field shape review vs epic contract table, recorded in receipt. Closes full epic TC table.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/team-impl-log.md:172:- Test plan: mock nothing; real SQLite per-test temp dirs; injected fixed clock; ~72 tests expected. Test titles carry TC ids. CLI process suite only in verify-all (labeled skip in verify). TC-1.6 compensation via parent-is-regular-file.\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:63:// proof of durability (anti-shim requirement).\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:112:      workItemId: \"w-m2-tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:114:      kind: \"tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:130:        workItemId: \"w-m2-tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:132:        kind: \"tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:164:      original, // duplicate: skipped\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:172:        outcome: \"skipped\",\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:173:        skipReason: \"duplicate_idempotency_key\",\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:267:  it(\"TC-5.4 (no-work-item clause): a skipped event queues nothing (AC-5.4)\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:277:    expect(resend.events.map((entry) => entry.outcome)).toEqual([\"skipped\", \"skipped\"]);\npackages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts:307:        { work_item_id: \"w-m3-tool_result_summary\", status: \"queued\" },\npackages/lhc/test/thread-migration.test.ts:111:  it(\"idempotency keys recorded under Story 2 still skip after the upgrade\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/thread-migration.test.ts:125:      outcome: \"skipped\",\npackages/lhc/test/thread-migration.test.ts:126:      skipReason: \"duplicate_idempotency_key\",\npackages/lhc/test/thread-migration.test.ts:129:    // No message was created for the skipped resend of the legacy event.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:7:The system under test is the lhc package entered through its two real entry points: SDK operations and CLI commands. Internal modules — pipeline, projection, stores, registry — are exercised through those entries, never tested in isolation against mocked neighbors. Two deliberate exceptions get supplemental pure-function suites because they are genuinely pure logic with contractual behavior: the turn state machine (golden cases mirroring the epic's rule table) and token estimation (golden counts). Everything else earns its coverage through entry-point behavior.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:9:**What gets mocked: nothing.** This epic has no network, no inference provider, no clock-sensitive contract beyond injected `clock`, and its filesystem behavior *is* the product (durability, atomicity, restart survival). Every test runs against real SQLite files in a per-test temp directory. The injected clock is fixed per test for deterministic `recordedAt`/`queuedAt`/`createdAt`; the tokenizer runs real because golden-counting real output is stronger than stubbing it.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:17:| CLI process boundary | vitest, spawns `dist/cli.js` | real argv/stdin/exit codes | `verify-all` only (labeled skip otherwise) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:55:### Flow 2 — `intake.test.ts` (recording), `projection.test.ts` (messages)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:60:| TC-2.2 | per-kind projection | `eventBatch` with all seven kinds | 6 messages (no turn_end); block types per design's mapping table; content verbatim |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:65:| TC-2.7 | message work queued | prompt + tool_result batch | two items: `w-m1-prompt_smoothing`, `w-m2-tool_result_summary`; owner messages; status queued |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:99:| TC-5.1 | full resend | record 5, resend same 5 | zero recorded; five skips with `duplicate_idempotency_key`; read-back unchanged |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:100:| TC-5.2 | partial resend | 3 recorded + 2 new | three skips, two recorded; orders continue dense (no gaps from skips) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:102:| TC-5.4 | skips inert | resend recorded prompt + turn_end mid-conversation | turn count/states unchanged; no transitions in result; work-item count unchanged; no duplicate message |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:103:| TC-5.5 | key wins | key K with payload A recorded; resend K with valid payload B | skipped; read-back returns A; B nowhere in any table (`openRaw` scan) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/test-plan.md:115:| Atomicity under mid-walk failure | induced failure inside the transaction (test seam closes handle) → read-back equals baseline | TC-4.3 covers validation rejection; this covers in-transaction failure |\npackages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts:42:      reason: `not implemented: ${op}`,\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:12:LHC keeps the full history of an agentic conversation as a durable record and builds summarized working views from it. This epic builds the record's front half: threads come into existence, and harness activity flows into them.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:33:- [Flow 2: Event Batch Intake](#flow-2-event-batch-intake) — recording, message projection, token estimates, message-level work queueing. AC-2.1–2.9\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:43:**Key constraint:** Intake sits in the harness's hot path, so it must be local, deterministic, and fast; binary outcomes with structured reasons, never partial landings.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:45:**Agents and developers** drive the CLI against a thread during integration work and verification: creating test threads, replaying captured sessions, reading back what landed.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:58:- Message and block projection from message-producing events, with token estimates stamped at creation\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:132:The envelope and event objects are strict contracts: an unknown field anywhere, on the envelope, on an event, or in a payload, is a validation failure unless this contract explicitly lists it. Nothing is silently dropped. Validation precedes idempotency: every event in the batch must pass shape validation before any skip decision is made. A duplicate key on a malformed event is a rejection, not a skip. Tool-result payloads are recorded in full at intake; any truncation or summarization of tool output is derived or rendering behavior belonging to later epics, never a property of the record.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:140:- **AC-2.5**: A `tool_result` payload is recorded and read back in full, regardless of size; intake never truncates or summarizes record content.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:142:- **AC-2.7**: The batch result reports, per event: recorded or skipped, and for recorded message-producing events the created message id; plus any turn transitions the batch caused and any work items it queued.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:143:- **AC-2.8**: A `user_prompt` message durably queues prompt-smoothing work and a `tool_result` message durably queues tool-result-summary work, each owned by `messages`, in the same atomic operation that records the batch.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:154:- **TC-2.7** (AC-2.8): A batch with a prompt and a tool result → one `messages`-owned work item for prompt smoothing and one for tool-result summary, readable from the queue with `queued` status and correct source references.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:234:A harness that crashed or timed out mid-send cannot know what landed. It resends the same batch. Every event carries a caller-chosen idempotency key, unique per thread; events whose keys are already recorded are skipped, the rest land normally, and the result says which was which.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:240:- **AC-5.1**: Resending a fully recorded batch records nothing, skips every event, and reports each skip with its idempotency key.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:241:- **AC-5.2**: Resending a batch where some events are recorded and some are new skips the recorded ones and records the new ones in batch order.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:243:- **AC-5.4**: A skipped event causes no side effects: no duplicate message, no turn transition, no work item.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:244:- **AC-5.5**: A valid event reusing a recorded key with different content is skipped; the original record is unchanged and the new content is not stored anywhere.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:248:- **TC-5.1** (AC-5.1): Record a five-event batch, resend it identically → zero recorded, five skips reported by key, each carrying `skipReason: duplicate_idempotency_key`; full read-back unchanged.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:249:- **TC-5.2** (AC-5.2): Resend with three old events plus two new → three skips, two recorded continuing the event order.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:252:- **TC-5.5** (AC-5.5): Key K recorded with payload A; resend key K with valid payload B → skipped, read-back still returns payload A, no new message or work item.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:319:| `tool_result` | `content` | string | yes | Recorded in full, never truncated at intake |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:330:| `events` | array | Per input event, in order: `{ idempotencyKey, outcome: recorded \\| skipped, messageId?, skipReason? }`. `skipReason` present exactly when skipped; sole value in this epic: `duplicate_idempotency_key` |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:352:| `kind` | string | `prompt_smoothing`, `tool_result_summary` (owner `messages`); `turn_derivation` (owner `turns`) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:397:Partitioning follows the pipeline rather than the flow list: each story adds one stage of the intake path and its read-back, so every story lands testable behavior on top of the previous one. The flow-per-story cut was considered and rejected — Flow 2 alone spans projection, tokens, and queueing, and validation (Flow 4) shares machinery with recording rather than standing alone.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:404:**Boundary / risk notes:** Stubs fail closed with typed errors, never fake success (tech arch convention).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:419:**Delivers:** `intake-stream message-events` recording events durably in order, full batch validation with all-or-nothing rejection, idempotency-keyed skips, event read-back. No messages, turns, or work items yet.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:423:**Flows/ACs covered:** AC-2.1, AC-2.9 (recording, empty batch); Flow 4 — AC-4.1 through AC-4.6 (corruption half of AC-4.7 arrives in Story 4); Flow 5 — AC-5.1 through AC-5.5 at the event level, including validation-before-skip precedence.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:431:**Boundary / risk notes:** Projection extends the existing atomic operation — a projection failure must reject the batch, not strand recorded events without messages.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:446:**Delivers:** Durable work items: message-level (prompt smoothing, tool-result summary, owned by `messages`) queued as qualifying messages land; turn-level (owned by `turns`) queued at both close paths; work-item read-back; the batch result complete with `queuedWork`.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/epic.md:449:**Boundary / risk notes:** This story fixes the contract seam Epic 02 builds against — owner, kind, sourceRef, and `queued` status must match what the derivation epic consumes. Work items must queue inside the batch's atomic operation (a rejected batch queues nothing, a skipped event queues nothing).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/01-thread-creation-registry-resolution.md:63:- Compensation must be exercised by a real registry failure (file-as-parent path), not a mocked insert error.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/01-thread-creation-registry-resolution.md:114:- TC-1.6's registry failure must be real (parent-is-a-regular-file path), exercising the actual compensation code — not a mocked insert error.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/01-thread-creation-registry-resolution.md:121:- Registration/default path: CLI router from Story 0 routes these commands to the real surface, replacing the fail-closed stubs.\npackages/lhc/test/smoke.test.ts:6:// (test/projection.test.ts), Story 4 turns list (test/turns.test.ts), and\npackages/lhc/test/smoke.test.ts:8:// stubbed commands remain, so the Story 0 fail-closed-stub rail test retired\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:26:The design inherits hard lessons from the predecessor MVP, and three of them shape almost every decision below. First: turn membership used to be inferred after the fact by scanning the stream, which made every downstream consumer carry reconstruction logic and made repair guesswork — so here, membership is stamped synchronously in the hot path by a small fixed state machine, and a closed turn is frozen forever. Second: the MVP silently dropped unrecognized fields at the boundary, which let malformed integrations run for days before anyone noticed — so here, validation is strict and closed (unknown fields anywhere are rejections), all-or-nothing per batch, and validation always precedes idempotency skip decisions. Third: derived state used to block progress when it went stale, the single worst failure mode in the old system — so this epic records and queues but never gates on derivation, and the work-item seam it hands Epic 02 carries owner and kind so that derivation health stays the owning domain's business forever after.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:57:No network, no daemon, no inference provider appears anywhere in this epic. The only external boundary is the filesystem, and it is part of the product contract (durability, restart survival, reopen-sees-same-truth), so tests use real SQLite files in temp directories rather than mocks. That single fact shapes the whole testing strategy: there is nothing to mock in this epic.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:65:| `messages` | Message/block projection, token stamping, message read-back, message-owned work queueing, queued-work read-back | Edits, search (Epic 04); derivation execution (Epic 02) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:70:| token counting (util) | Base-unit estimates via `o200k_base`, stamped at projection | Model-weight calibration (future) |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:92:        project.ts          // event → message + blocks projection\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:127:| `intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts` | The batch transaction: ordering, idempotency skips after validation, cross-domain coordination, result assembly | AC-2.1, AC-2.7, AC-4.6, AC-4.7, AC-5.1–5.5, AC-1.6 |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:129:| `messages/index.ts` (queue half) | Queue `prompt_smoothing` / `tool_result_summary` items for qualifying messages | AC-2.8 |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:145:A closed turn queues exactly one `turn_derivation` item. A qualifying message queues exactly one item per applicable kind (`prompt_smoothing` for prompts, `tool_result_summary` for tool results). If Epic 02's turn derivation wants internal fan-out (smoothing, projection as separate steps), it fans out on its own side of the seam; the contract stays one-item-per-source-per-kind. This keeps the seam minimal, makes work-item ids derivable from their source, and matches the v1 reference's one-trigger-per-turn model, which worked.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:155:`js-tiktoken` with `o200k_base`, as the tech arch names. The thread file records `token_estimator = \"js-tiktoken:o200k_base\"` in its metadata row at creation — implementation and base encoding both named, so a future implementation swap on the same base is distinguishable from a base change, so every stored estimate is permanently interpretable even if a future version changes the base. The counting function is pure and injected nowhere — it is deterministic, dependency-free, and called directly by projection (the operation context carries no tokenizer seam; there is nothing to vary in tests, and goldening real counts is the better assertion).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:192:| Work item | `w-` + source id + `-` + kind | `w-m7-tool_result_summary`, `w-t2-turn_derivation` |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:210:### 9. Test substrate: real SQLite in temp directories, nothing mocked\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:212:The filesystem is the product contract in this epic — durability, restart survival, rejection-leaves-no-trace, reopen-sees-same-truth are all real-storage assertions. Every test gets a temp directory (registry + thread files), exercises the system through SDK operations or spawned/in-process CLI, and asserts on read-back. The What-Gets-Mocked table in the test plan is one row long: nothing. The clock is injected (not mocked — a fixed `clock` in the context is the real interface), and the tokenizer runs real because goldening true counts is strictly better than stubbing them.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:218:The system view above says who owns what; this section says how each flow actually moves. One structural fact organizes everything: **Flows 2 through 5 are not four code paths.** They are four contractual views of a single operation — `intake-stream.messageEvents` — whose internal pipeline is: validate the whole batch (pure, no database), open the transaction, walk events in order (skip duplicates, record, project, apply turn transition, queue work), assemble the result, commit. Validation failures reject before the transaction opens; anything that fails inside it rolls back whole. Flow 1 alone is a separate operation set on `threads`.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:260:TC-1.6's forced failure is induced by pointing `registryPath` at a path whose parent is an existing regular file — creating a database there fails identically on every platform and privilege level (a read-only directory does not: root and many CI sandboxes ignore permission bits). The failure is real, after a real file creation, exercising real compensation — no mocking, per Design Decision 9.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:264:The walk is where most of this epic's behavior lives. Each recorded event takes its order number from a counter initialized at `MAX(event_order)` when the transaction begins — skipped duplicates do not consume numbers, so the stream stays dense and AC-2.1's continuity holds across batches and across skips. Projection happens in the same iteration as recording: the message row, its blocks, the token estimate, and the actor/harness carry are written while the event is in hand, and the message id is the event's order number with an `m` prefix (Design Decision 7), so projection cannot drift from its source.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:277:    I->>I: skip if idempotency key recorded (AC-5.1–5.5)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:288:Two projection details are contractual rather than implementation-free. Block structure by kind: text-bearing kinds (`user_prompt`, `assistant_text`, `assistant_thinking`, `runtime_note`) project one text block; `tool_call` projects one block carrying `toolCallId`, `toolName`, and `arguments`; `tool_result` projects one block carrying `toolCallId`, the full `content`, and `isError`. Nothing splits, summarizes, or normalizes content at intake — AC-2.5's full preservation is the absence of any code path that could shorten it, which is why TC-2.4's hundreds-of-KB round-trip is a meaningful regression tripwire rather than a formality. Token estimates count each message's text content (for tool calls, the serialized arguments; for tool results, the full content string) through the one `token-counting` util, and the estimate lands on the message row in the same insert.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:290:The batch result is assembled during the walk, not reconstructed after it: each event appends its `{ idempotencyKey, outcome, messageId?, skipReason? }` entry as it is processed, turn transitions append as they fire, and work items append as they queue. The result a caller sees is therefore a faithful log of what the transaction did, in the order it did it — which TC-2.6 asserts for the mixed case.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:295:| TC-2.2 | Per-kind projection, turn_end exception | Block mapping table; no-message path |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:297:| TC-2.4 | Full tool-result fidelity | No-truncation projection path |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:321:The surface operation `turns.applyEvent` interprets the effect against storage: `open` inserts a turn row (`t<order>`, status `open`, opened-at-event-order) and returns the id for stamping; `close` updates status and closed-at and queues `w-t<order>-turn_derivation`; `close_then_open` does both in sequence, and the prompt that caused it stamps to the *new* turn (AC-3.3). Stamping itself is a column on the message row, written during projection using the turn id current *after* the transition — prompts transition first then stamp, which is the ordering that makes AC-3.1/3.3 come out right, while non-transition kinds stamp to whatever is open or null (AC-3.2, AC-3.8). A closed turn is never updated again by any code path in this epic; frozenness (AC-3.7) is the absence of a writer, verified by TC-3.5's read-back rather than enforced by a guard.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:323:Membership read-back (`memberMessageIds`) is a query over messages' `turn_id` column ordered by event order — membership is stored on the member, not as a list on the turn, so there is exactly one source of truth and the frozen guarantee reduces to \"messages never change their stamp,\" which projection's write-once design already gives.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:354:Deduplication is a `SELECT idempotency_key FROM event WHERE idempotency_key IN (…)` at transaction start, producing the skip set for the walk. A skipped event emits its result entry with `skipReason: \"duplicate_idempotency_key\"` and is otherwise invisible: no event row, no message, no order-number consumption, and — the consequence that matters most — **no turn transition**, because the walk's transition step only runs for recorded events. A resent prompt cannot re-close a turn; a resent `turn_end` cannot close whatever happens to be open now (TC-5.4's exact assertions). Key scoping is per-thread by construction: the lookup runs against the thread file, and the same key in another thread is another file (AC-5.3, no code needed).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:356:Content under a reused key is not compared (AC-5.5): the skip decision reads only the key column. This is stated as a design fact rather than left implicit — the original record wins, the resent content is never parsed past validation, and TC-5.5's payload-B-vanishes assertion is the proof.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:360:| TC-5.1 | Full resend all-skip | Skip set covers batch; result reports reasons |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:361:| TC-5.2 | Partial resend | Mixed walk; order continues from MAX, skips consume nothing |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:364:| TC-5.5 | Key wins over content | Key-only skip decision |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:462:    outcome: \"recorded\" | \"skipped\";\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:464:    skipReason?: \"duplicate_idempotency_key\";\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:536:export type WorkKind = \"prompt_smoothing\" | \"tool_result_summary\" | \"turn_derivation\";\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:595:**Risk shape:** four archetypes in one — pure types (error/result vocabulary), fixtures (builders + temp stores), command rail (CLI routing with structured-failure stubs), substrate (sqlite open/migrate, boundary script, verification scripts).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:596:**Delivers:** `shared/` complete (errors, context, storage); `tech-utils/token-counting` complete (it is pure and small — no reason to stub what costs nothing to finish); CLI rail routing all commands to fail-closed stubs returning `{ ok: false, error: { errorClass: \"system_error\", code: \"storage_failure\", reason: \"not implemented: <op>\" } }`; fixture builders (`validEvent(kind, overrides?)`, `eventBatch(…)`, temp-dir factory, `openRaw(path)` for below-SDK assertions); all four verification scripts runnable.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:597:**Exit criteria:** `verify-all` passes with zero behavior tests; smoke tests prove the rail (help, unknown command, stub failure shape), fixture validity (builders satisfy the Flow 4 schemas once they exist — initially golden-shaped), tokenizer determinism (golden counts for known strings), and boundary script failure on a deliberate violation (test-only sabotage file, then removed).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:610:**Risk shape:** the strict boundary and the transaction skeleton — the two highest-risk seams in the epic land together here, deliberately, before any projection complicates the walk.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:611:**Delivers:** Flow 4 complete (three-layer closed schemas, error mapping with `eventIndex`); Flow 5 complete at event level (skip set, `skipReason`, density of order numbers); Flow 2's recording half (the transaction, `MAX(event_order)` counter, event rows, walk-time result assembly minus messages/turns/work); `listEvents`; closes TC-1.4.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:614:**Architecture-risk tests:** atomicity under induced mid-walk failure (close the db handle mid-transaction via test seam — rollback leaves read-back at baseline); restart survival (write, close handle, reopen, read-back identical).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:619:**Delivers:** `createFromEvent` wired into the walk; per-kind block projection; token stamping via the Chunk 0 util; actor/harness carry; `listMessages`; deterministic `m<order>` ids; messageId in batch results.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:626:**Delivers:** `state-machine.ts` with golden cases per the epic's rule table; `applyEvent` against storage; stamping order (prompts stamp post-transition, others stamp to current-or-null); corruption check at state load; `listTurns` with membership-by-query; turn transitions in batch results. Work queueing on close is *not* here — Chunk 4 ships close paths that work completely and simply do not call the queue yet; Chunk 5 adds the queue call to those working paths inside the same transaction. Nothing is stubbed or faked at the seam, and no close fails for lack of queueing; Chunk 4's close tests assert transitions and membership only.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:634:**Delivers:** `tech-utils/work-queue` (recordItem, listItems); `queueMessageWork` (kind gate: prompt/tool_result only); turn-close queueing through the Chunk 4 seam; deterministic `w-` ids; `listQueuedWork` on both owning domains; `queuedWork` in batch results; work items inside the batch transaction (rejected batch queues nothing, skipped event queues nothing).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:646:Strictly linear, matching the epic's story graph. The two named deferrals (Chunk 1→2: TC-1.4; Chunk 4→5: work-item assertions in TC-3.3/3.6) are the only cross-chunk test debts, both recorded in the chunk that owes them and asserted closed in the chunk that pays them. No chunk mocks a neighbor; the seam between Chunk 4 and 5 is an absent call added later, neither stubbed nor faked.\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process-projection.test.ts:1:// CLI process-boundary suite for message projection: TC-2.4's spawned-CLI\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process-projection.test.ts:3:// projection) and comes back byte-identical through `messages list`, proving\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process-projection.test.ts:34:  dir = mkdtempSync(path.join(tmpdir(), \"lhc-proc-projection-\"));\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process-projection.test.ts:50:describe(\"Story 3 (process boundary): projection through the spawned binary\", () => {\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/04-turn-state-machine.md:23:**Scope — out:** Turn-close work queueing. This story implements turn close and membership completely — closes succeed and are fully usable. It does not invoke or fake any work-queue behavior: the close path simply does not call the queue yet. Story 5 adds queueing to these already-working close paths inside the same batch transaction. TC-3.3/TC-3.6's work-item assertions are Story 5's named debt; nothing in this story stubs, fakes, or fails on their behalf.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/04-turn-state-machine.md:48:- **AC-3.6** is **not claimed by this story**. This story proves the close-path transition and membership behavior only. It does not invoke, stub, or fake queueing — the queue call simply does not exist in the close paths yet. Story 5 adds the turn-derivation work item to these already-working close paths and owns AC-3.6 and the work-item halves of TC-3.3/TC-3.6.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/04-turn-state-machine.md:57:Turn close works completely in this story and queues nothing — the queue call simply does not exist yet; Story 5 adds it to these working paths inside the same transaction. Nothing is stubbed or faked at that seam.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/04-turn-state-machine.md:153:- [ ] Turn close fully working with no queue invocation — no stub called, no fake item, no failure injected; queueing arrives in Story 5\npackages/lhc/test/red-manifest.json:10:    \"test/projection.test.ts\": \"35c1b3fa2a45932df8f92a743d0f47f2b769aa5cda316c5f4e47483cc02ae039\",\npackages/lhc/test/red-manifest.json:11:    \"test/cli-process-projection.test.ts\": \"ef4770eacb01448c0414a4316ed0b1be17d45b42d2fa6dbead26f496f93b35e3\",\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process.test.ts:2:// verify-all only (needs a build artifact); plain verify announces the skip.\npackages/lhc/test/cli-process.test.ts:46:    // stub), so the rail proof is now the real error path: an unknown thread\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:47:  it(\"TC-5.1: resending a fully recorded batch skips everything and changes nothing\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:62:      expect(entry.outcome).toBe(\"skipped\");\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:63:      expect(entry.skipReason).toBe(\"duplicate_idempotency_key\");\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:71:  it(\"TC-5.2: partial resend skips the old, records the new, and keeps the order dense\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:82:      \"skipped\",\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:83:      \"skipped\",\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:84:      \"skipped\",\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:113:  it(\"TC-5.4: skips are inert — no duplicate rows, no order numbers consumed, no transitions reported\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:122:    expect(resend.value.events.every((entry) => entry.outcome === \"skipped\")).toBe(true);\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:164:      outcome: \"skipped\",\npackages/lhc/test/idempotency.test.ts:165:      skipReason: \"duplicate_idempotency_key\",\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/coverage.md:62:**TC-5.4 clause ladder** (skipped events cause no side effects — each clause asserted when its record kind exists):\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/coverage.md:78:| Rejected/skipped batch queues nothing; final full-surface regression — rejected batch leaves events, messages, turns, and work items at baseline (complete-surface TC-4.3) | 5 |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/coverage.md:90:    dedup-check (skip set, key-only) .......... S2\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/coverage.md:100:End-to-end integration test exists from Story 2 onward (create → batch → read-back) and gains assertions each story; Story 5's DoD requires the full epic TC table green, which is the integration gate.\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:1:// Story 3 (Flow 2, projection half): TC-2.2 through TC-2.5, TC-5.4's\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:2:// no-duplicate-message clause, and the projection rung of the rollback ladder\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:3:// (an induced projection failure rejects the whole batch). Everything enters\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:5:// in cli-process-projection.test.ts.\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:52:describe(\"Flow 2 (SDK): message projection\", () => {\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:56:      validEvent(\"user_prompt\", { payload: { text: \"please summarize the file\" } }),\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:124:      { blockType: \"text\", content: { text: \"please summarize the file\" } },\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:242:  it(\"TC-5.4 (message clause): a skipped event creates no duplicate message (AC-5.4)\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:253:    expect(resend.value.events.every((entry) => entry.outcome === \"skipped\")).toBe(true);\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:271:  it(\"architecture-risk: an induced projection failure rejects the whole batch — no recorded events without messages\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:281:    // the seam drops the block table, so the second event's projection insert\npackages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts:297:    // both at baseline — projection failure stranded no event rows.\npackages/lhc/test/turns.test.ts:289:    expect(resend.events.map((entry) => entry.outcome)).toEqual([\"skipped\", \"skipped\"]);\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:13:**Objective:** Everything recorded becomes readable, with its size known. Each message-producing event yields exactly one message with kind-appropriate typed blocks, verbatim content, the actor/harness carry, and a deterministic base-unit token estimate — written in the same walk iteration that records the event, so projection can never drift from its source.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:17:- Per-kind block projection: text kinds → one text block; `tool_call` → one block (`toolCallId`, `toolName`, `arguments`); `tool_result` → one block (`toolCallId`, full `content`, `isError`); `turn_end` → no message\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:21:- Projection failure rejects the batch (extends the transaction's all-or-nothing to projection)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:36:- **AC-2.5**: A `tool_result` payload is recorded and read back in full, regardless of size; intake never truncates or summarizes record content.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:40:- **AC-5.4** (message clause): A skipped event creates no duplicate message.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:48:This story adds projection to Story 2's walk: each recorded message-producing event yields its message, blocks, and token estimate in the same iteration that recorded it, inside the same transaction — projection cannot drift from its source because they are never separated. The risk profile is fidelity, not logic: kind-correct block shapes, verbatim content, and the absence of any code path that could shorten a tool result. `messages.createFromEvent` is the first cross-domain surface call through the operation context, establishing the pattern `turns.applyEvent` follows in Story 4.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:58:- Verbatim means verbatim: no trimming, normalizing, or summarizing anywhere in the projection path (AC-2.5 is the absence of such code).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:59:- A projection failure must reject the whole batch — recorded events without messages is the stranded state the transaction exists to prevent.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:71:| Tests | `test/projection.test.ts` |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:75:- [02-tech-design.md §Flow 2 (projection details + block mapping)](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/02-tech-design.md:288), lines 288–303\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:85:| TC-2.2 | `test/projection.test.ts` | all seven kinds → six messages, kind-correct blocks, verbatim; turn_end absent |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:86:| TC-2.3 | `test/projection.test.ts` | identical content, two threads → identical estimates; estimate on every message |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:87:| TC-2.4 | `test/projection.test.ts` + process suite | 300KB tool result → byte-identical read-back, SDK and spawned CLI |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:88:| TC-2.5 | `test/projection.test.ts` | actor/harness carried unchanged onto messages |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:90:TC-5.4's no-duplicate-message clause is re-asserted here now that messages exist (clause ladder per `coverage.md`). The rollback ladder gains its projection rung: an induced projection failure rejects the whole batch.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:96:| Projection failure strands nothing | `test/projection.test.ts` | induced projection failure → whole batch rejected, no event rows without messages | TCs exercise happy projection; the stranded-state hazard only appears on the failure path |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:102:- Token estimates: tool calls count serialized `arguments`; tool results count the full `content` string; same insert as the message row; the util is called directly (no seam — golden counts beat stubs).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:109:- The projection-failure test induces failure through a real mechanism, and the assertion is read-back state, not the error result alone.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:114:- Registration/default path: CLI router to the real messages surface; projection runs inside the production intake pipeline, not a separate path.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:115:- Evidence: TC-2.4's spawned-CLI leg exercises stdin → pipeline → projection → read-back end to end.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:119:- Targeted: `pnpm test -- test/projection.test.ts`\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/03-message-projection-tokens.md:134:- [ ] Projection failure rejects the whole batch (supplemental test, induced via test seam)\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:6:The walking skeleton everything else lands on: package scaffold, error/result vocabulary, fixture builders, CLI rail with fail-closed stubs, and the four verification gates.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:19:- CLI rail: command routing for all planned commands, each landing on a fail-closed stub returning `{ ok: false, error: { errorClass: \"system_error\", code: \"storage_failure\", reason: \"not implemented: <op>\" } }`; `--help`; unknown-command handling; JSON rendering; exit codes\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:21:- Verification gates: `red-verify`, `verify`, `green-verify`, `verify-all` runnable; `scripts/check-boundaries.mjs` enforcing import rules (fixtures exempt); CLI process suite scaffold with labeled skip under plain `verify`\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:24:**Scope — out:** All product behavior. No thread creation, no intake, no projection, no turns, no work items. Stubs fail closed; nothing fakes success.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:34:- **FC-0.2**: `verify-all` passes with zero behavior tests; the CLI process suite's absence from plain `verify` prints a labeled skip, never silent\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:35:- **FC-0.3**: CLI responds to `--help`; unknown commands exit non-zero with a structured error; every planned command routes to a fail-closed stub with the exact stub error shape\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:46:This story builds the structure every other story lands behavior in: the domain-surface module tree with enforced import boundaries, the error/result vocabulary every operation speaks, the fixture builders every test arranges with, and the four verification gates every phase exits through. Nothing here is product behavior — the CLI routes to fail-closed stubs and the SDK exports types and one finished util (token counting, which is pure and costs nothing to complete now).\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:55:- Mostly scaffold with obvious shape; TDD against stubs that intentionally fail would be circular.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:58:- Stubs must fail closed with the exact typed error shape — a stub returning fake success poisons every later story's red phase.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:93:| FC-0.2 | `pnpm run verify-all` | passes with zero behavior tests; labeled CLI-suite skip under `verify` |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:94:| FC-0.3 | `test/smoke.test.ts` | help, unknown command, stub failure shape |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:117:- Stub error shape is asserted exactly (`system_error` / `storage_failure` / `not implemented: <op>`) — not just \"an error happened.\"\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:125:- Evidence: process-suite legs (`--help`, unknown command, stub shape) spawn the built binary; `verify-all` includes them.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/stories/00-foundation.md:142:- [ ] All planned CLI commands route to fail-closed stubs; no stub returns fake success\npackages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts:49:// projection failure propagates to the pipeline's catch and rejects the\npackages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts:78:// result queues tool_result_summary, nothing else queues anything — text,\npackages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts:85:  tool_result: \"tool_result_summary\",\npackages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/index.ts:36:    outcome: \"recorded\" | \"skipped\";\npackages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/index.ts:38:    skipReason?: \"duplicate_idempotency_key\";\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/artifacts/quick-fix/001-quick-fix.json:1:{\"command\":\"quick-fix\",\"version\":1,\"status\":\"ok\",\"outcome\":\"ready-for-verification\",\"result\":{\"provider\":\"claude-code\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\",\"rawProviderOutputPreview\":\"{\\\"type\\\":\\\"system\\\",\\\"subtype\\\":\\\"init\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7d47137f-cd86-4c15-bbf8-4a8dc794929e\\\",\\\"tools\\\":[\\\"Task\\\",\\\"AskUserQuestion\\\",\\\"Bash\\\",\\\"CronCreate\\\",\\\"CronDelete\\\",\\\"CronList\\\",\\\"Edit\\\",\\\"EnterPlanMode\\\",\\\"EnterWorktree\\\",\\\"ExitPlanMode\\\",\\\"ExitWorktree\\\",\\\"Monitor\\\",\\\"NotebookEdit\\\",\\\"PushNotification\\\",\\\"Read\\\",\\\"RemoteTrigger\\\",\\\"ScheduleWakeup\\\",\\\"Skill\\\",\\\"TaskCreate\\\",\\\"TaskGet\\\",\\\"TaskList\\\",\\\"TaskOutput\\\",\\\"TaskStop\\\",\\\"TaskUpdate\\\",\\\"ToolSearch\\\",\\\"WebFetch\\\",\\\"WebSearch\\\",\\\"Workflow\\\",\\\"Write\\\",\\\"mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate\\\",\\\"mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication\\\",\\\"mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate\\\",\\\"mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authenticat