{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":472738,"duration_api_ms":473718,"ttft_ms":12918,"ttft_stream_ms":10382,"time_to_request_ms":8,"num_turns":45,"result":"All checks are complete: both gates pass (verify-all: 16 files / 111 tests, exit 0; green-verify including test-immutability: exit 0), every TC from the test plan is present and substantively asserted (raw-SQL durability proofs, full-surface rollback diffs, below-SDK absence scans), and the production path is real SQLite end to end. The findings below capture the few drifts I found.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"outcome\": \"pass\",\n  \"crossStoryFindings\": [\n    \"The five stories compose into one coherent pipeline exactly as designed: validate (pure, pre-lock) → BEGIN IMMEDIATE → corruption check → per-event [dedup → record → turn transition → projection with membership stamp → work queueing] → walk-time result assembly → COMMIT (src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts:84-254). The transition-before-stamp ordering is implemented and TC-3.8's two-turns-in-one-batch case works without lookahead.\",\n    \"Deterministic id scheme is consistent across stories: m<eventOrder> (messages/index.ts:63), t<turnOrder> (turns/internal/store.ts:27), w-<sourceId>-<kind> (tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:40), matching tech-design decision 7; work-item ids are naturally idempotent for the Epic 02 repair path.\",\n    \"The error model (OpResult, three classes, stable codes) is uniform across all four domain surfaces and the CLI; each class has exactly one producer (validation → caller_error, corruption check → state_corruption, wrapped SQLite/fs → system_error) as the design requires, and TC-4.4/turns tests assert all three classes against each other through both SDK and CLI.\",\n    \"Later stories' tests build their substrate through earlier stories' real surfaces (threadWith-style helpers create threads via newThread and record via messageEvents), giving the continuous upstream re-verification the design called for; no story mocks a neighbor.\",\n    \"Minor inconsistency: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92-93, 267-268), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations skip validateThreadRef entirely — an unknown field on a read-back thread reference is silently tolerated there. The epic's closed-contract rule formally binds only the intake envelope, so this is drift in rigor, not a contract violation.\"\n  ],\n  \"architectureFindings\": [\n    \"Module structure matches the tech design's layout exactly (domains/{threads,intake-stream,messages,turns} with index.ts + internal/, tech-utils/{work-queue,token-counting}, shared/{context,errors,storage}, cli/{index,render}, sdk.ts); the boundary check (scripts/check-boundaries.mjs) enforces both rules with the fixtures exemption and passes over 38 files.\",\n    \"Import boundaries hold: all cross-domain calls go through domain index surfaces; the only below-SDK reach is test/fixtures/ (corrupt.ts, intake-seam.ts, legacy.ts), which is the design's sanctioned exemption.\",\n    \"Beyond-design addition (sound): the thread-file schema is a versioned migration history (v1 events, v2 messages, v3 turns, v4 work items) with lazy upgrade through openThreadDatabase and identity validation before any migration (threads/internal/create.ts:28-190). This is not in the tech design but is consistent with file-is-authority, is well-tested (F-03-001/F-03-002, thread-migration.test.ts), and protects pre-story files.\",\n    \"Interface drift (benign): messages.createFromEvent takes a third turnId parameter (messages/index.ts:55-59) versus the design's two-arg signature — the natural realization of the design's own stamp-after-transition rule; turns.applyEvent matches the design signature exactly.\",\n    \"Contract drift against the epic: Flow 4 declares 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' invalid, but validate.ts accepts empty-string text (user_prompt/assistant_text/assistant_thinking/runtime_note) and empty tool_result.content (validate.ts:55-65 uses Schema.String, not NonEmptyString), while rejecting empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey. The tech design's interfaces typed these as plain string, so the drift originates between epic and design. See non-blocking finding NB-1.\",\n    \"Transaction boundaries, BEGIN IMMEDIATE, file-then-row creation with compensation, registry lazy-init with non-creating reads, tokenizer pinning (js-tiktoken:o200k_base recorded in thread metadata at creation), and the operation-context shape all match the design decisions 2, 3, 6, and 8 as written.\"\n  ],\n  \"epicCoverageAssessment\": [\n    \"All 33 TCs from the epic appear as greppably-tagged tests and pass: Flow 1 (TC-1.1–1.6 with CLI legs), Flow 2 (TC-2.1–2.9), Flow 3 (TC-3.1–3.8 plus the golden state-machine suite covering every rule-table row), Flow 4 (TC-4.1–4.5 plus the unknown-field and empty actor/harness supplementals), Flow 5 (TC-5.1–5.5 with the below-SDK rawDump absence scan for TC-5.5).\",\n    \"Both named cross-chunk deferrals are explicitly closed in their owing stories: TC-1.4 id/path equivalence lives in intake.test.ts titled 'closes Story 1's deferral', and the TC-3.3/TC-3.6 work halves are paid in work-queue.test.ts titled 'Story 4's debt paid'.\",\n    \"All eight architecture-risk rows from the test plan exist and pass: mid-walk rollback via the induced-failure seam, restart survival extended to work items, rejected-batch-takes-no-lock, system_error rollback parity, fixture validity, boundary-check coverage, registry lazy-init non-creation, plus migration coverage beyond the plan.\",\n    \"Test quality is substantive, not title-deep: durability asserted through raw SQL row scans (work-queue.test.ts:64-72, 300-310), rejection asserted through full five-surface logical read-back diffs against baselines, and the 300KB tool-result round-trip runs through both SDK and spawned-binary stdin.\",\n    \"111 tests across 16 files versus the plan's ~72 estimate — the overage is migration tests, CLI-process splits per story, and supplemental strictness cases, all in-scope.\",\n    \"Gates: verify-all (epic gate) passes end to end including the spawned-binary CLI process suite; green-verify (story gate) passes including the Red-manifest test-immutability check (33+ red-phase files unchanged).\"\n  ],\n  \"productionPathFindings\": [\n    \"No fake adapters, mocks, or stubbed success exist anywhere on the production path: storage is real node:sqlite (WAL, foreign keys, busy_timeout — shared/storage.ts:8-15), token counting is real js-tiktoken o200k_base, and every CLI command body is one SDK call plus JSON rendering. The test suite's what-gets-mocked-nothing claim holds in the code.\",\n    \"Two module-level test seams live in the production pipeline module: setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:21-36). Both are null/no-op in production, settable only through the boundary-exempt fixtures directory, and fail safe (real wall clock when unset; explicit clock argument wins). The walk hook is design-sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row; the clock setter is an implementation addition beyond the design's context-injected clock. Reported as material per the audit mandate; neither fakes success or alters production behavior.\",\n    \"Dead code: shared/errors.ts:36-45 notImplemented() is the Story 0 fail-closed stub vocabulary with no remaining callers.\",\n    \"Stale build artifacts in dist/ from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) are not produced by the current src/ and would ship under files:[\\\"dist\\\"] if the package were ever published (it is private). The bin target dist/cli.js is current and rebuilt by every gate run.\",\n    \"newThread stamps createdAt from the raw wall clock with no injection seam (threads/index.ts:85), a small departure from the test plan's claim that the injected clock covers createdAt; no test needed it and production behavior is correct.\"\n  ],\n  \"blockingFindings\": [],\n  \"nonBlockingFindings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NB-1\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"title\": \"Empty-string payload text/content accepted despite epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Epic Flow 4 lists 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' as invalid. validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63), so { text: \\\"\\\" } and empty tool_result content pass validation, while empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey are rejected via NonEmptyString. The tech design's interface section typed these as plain string, so design and epic disagree; the implementation followed the design. Whether an empty tool result (a real occurrence — tools legitimately return empty output) should be a rejection is a product call.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/validate.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-4.2\", \"AC-4.4\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NB-2\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"title\": \"CLI parses flags with strict:false, silently ignoring unknown or misspelled flags\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cli/index.ts:53-64 calls parseArgs with strict: false, so `lhc threads new-thread --file-path p --titel x` creates the thread with no title and no error. This is the silent-drop failure mode the epic's boundary philosophy ('Nothing is silently dropped') exists to prevent, though the closed-contract rule formally covers the event envelope, not argv. A strict: true parse with a structured usage error would align the CLI boundary with the SDK boundary's strictness.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-4.7\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NB-3\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Read-back surfaces skip closed thread-reference validation\",\n      \"evidence\": \"messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92, 267), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations pass the reference straight to resolveThreadRef, so an unknown field or empty filePath on a read-back call is tolerated (empty path surfaces as thread_not_found rather than invalid_event). Behavior is still fail-closed; the inconsistency is in which error the caller gets and whether unknown ref fields are named.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\", \"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.6\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NB-4\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Mutable test seams exported from the production pipeline module\",\n      \"evidence\": \"setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:21-36) are module-global mutable state in production code, reachable by any importer of the internal module path even though the in-repo boundary check confines them to test/fixtures and the package export map exposes only the SDK root. Unset they are inert. Consider relocating behind a single test-only entry or documenting them in the design's seam inventory when Epic 02 extends the context.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts\", \"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/intake-seam.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NB-5\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Dead stub helper and stale dist artifacts left from earlier phases\",\n      \"evidence\": \"shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no callers since the Story 0 CLI stubs were replaced. dist/ retains compiled output from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) because tsc does not clean; the package is private so nothing ships, but a clean step or removing the strays would prevent confusion about what the production build contains.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts\", \"packages/lhc/dist\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"unresolvedItems\": [\n    \"Whether epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule applies to payload text/content fields (NB-1): the epic prose says reject, the tech design's typed interfaces say accept, and the implementation follows the design. Needs a human ruling and an erratum on whichever document loses.\",\n    \"The work_item table has no UNIQUE/status constraints beyond the primary key by design (Epic 02 adds lifecycle); confirm Epic 02's schema migration owns adding the status vocabulary constraint so 'queued'-only is not silently relied on.\"\n  ],\n  \"gateResult\": \"pass\"\n}\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"eae81ce6-d2f3-4109-b063-3fc436551a6a","total_cost_usd":4.6084724999999995,"usage":{"input_tokens":4120,"cache_creation_input_tokens":135007,"cache_read_input_tokens":1695036,"output_tokens":23662,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":135007,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8027,"cache_read_input_tokens":146973,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3852,"cache_creation":